# NOBORU FRONTEND RULE

Version: 1.0

Priority: CRITICAL

Status: AUTHORITATIVE

This rule governs all frontend implementation within Noboru.

Every page, component, layout, hook used for presentation, and client interaction must follow this document.

If this rule conflicts with architecture.mdc on layering, architecture.mdc takes precedence.

For visual design, motion, and screen behavior, defer to uiux.mdc.

⸻

PURPOSE

The purpose of this rule is to ensure:

* Consistent Next.js 15 and React 19 patterns
* Mobile-first, premium UI aligned with Noboru identity
* Clear separation between presentation and business logic
* Reusable feature-scoped components
* Design token consistency across the application
* Long-term maintainability of the UI layer

The frontend must feel like Noboru on every screen.

It must never become a collection of one-off pages.

⸻

REQUIREMENTS

Stack

* Next.js 15 App Router
* React 19
* TypeScript strict mode
* Tailwind CSS
* ShadCN UI components
* Inter as the primary typeface

Structure

* Feature UI lives in features/*/components/
* Shared UI primitives live in components/ or lib/ as established by the codebase
* Pages in app/ orchestrate workflows only
* Hooks in features/*/hooks/ may coordinate UI state but must not contain business logic

Layering

* UI calls services or application-layer hooks
* UI must never access repositories or the database directly
* UI must never embed business rules that belong in services

Design System

* Use CSS variables and tokens defined in app/globals.css
* Use semantic tokens: background, foreground, primary, secondary, muted, destructive, border, ring, radius
* Support light and dark themes via existing token structure
* Do not introduce hardcoded hex colors when a token exists

Mobile First

* Design and implement for mobile viewports first
* Desktop layouts adapt from mobile, never the reverse
* Primary actions must remain thumb-reachable

Pages

* Pages compose feature components
* Pages handle routing, layout, and workflow entry
* Pages must not contain validation, scoring, persistence, or domain calculations

Offline Presentation

* Core learning UI must support loading, empty, error, offline, and recovery states
* UI must not assume network availability for core learning flows

⸻

CODING STANDARDS

Components

* Prefer function components with explicit TypeScript props
* Keep components focused on one responsibility
* Extract reusable UI into feature or shared components
* Use ShadCN primitives before creating custom controls

Naming

* Components: PascalCase
* Files: match exported component name
* Feature components remain inside their feature module

Imports

* Prefer absolute imports using project aliases
* Do not create cross-feature UI dependencies unless through shared lib systems

Styling

* Use Tailwind utility classes with design tokens
* Use cn() or equivalent for conditional class merging
* Avoid inline styles except for dynamic values that cannot be expressed with tokens

Data Flow

* Presentation components receive data via props
* Container or page-level components fetch via services or application hooks
* Never fetch directly from Supabase inside .tsx files

Accessibility Baseline

* Every interactive element requires an accessible name
* Follow uiux.mdc and accessibility.mdc for keyboard, focus, and motion requirements

Performance Baseline

* Avoid unnecessary client components
* Prefer Server Components where interactivity is not required
* Lazy-load heavy client modules when appropriate

⸻

SUCCESS CRITERIA

A frontend change is successful when:

* The screen works on mobile first and scales cleanly to desktop
* Business logic remains outside pages and presentational components
* Design tokens from app/globals.css are used consistently
* Feature ownership is obvious from file location
* ShadCN and shared primitives are reused instead of duplicated
* Loading, error, empty, and offline states are handled
* A new developer can locate the correct feature component quickly

⸻

FAILURE CRITERIA

Frontend quality is degraded when any of the following occur:

* Business logic inside pages or presentational components
* Direct database or repository access from UI
* Hardcoded colors, spacing systems, or typography outside tokens
* One-off components duplicated across features
* Desktop-first layouts retrofitted onto mobile
* Educational content hardcoded into UI
* Missing error or offline states on core learning screens
* Feature UI imported directly across unrelated feature boundaries

When failure criteria appear, refactor before adding more UI on top.

⸻

EXAMPLES

Correct page orchestration

app/learning/page.tsx composes features/learning/components/LessonDashboard and passes route params only.

Correct feature component

features/vocabulary/components/VocabularyCard.tsx renders props, emits events, and contains no Supabase calls.

Correct token usage

className="bg-background text-foreground border-border"

Correct service usage

const items = await vocabularyService.getDueItems(userId);

Incorrect page logic

Calculating SRS intervals, validating answers, or writing progress inside app/review/page.tsx.

Incorrect styling

style={{ color: "#E23D4D" }} when --primary exists.

Incorrect data access

supabase.from("vocabulary").select("*") inside a component.

⸻

ANTI-PATTERNS

Database queries inside components or pages

Business logic inside pages

Business logic inside presentational hooks

Duplicated card, button, or modal implementations per feature

Hardcoded educational content in JSX

Hardcoded brand colors bypassing globals.css

Cross-feature component imports that create coupling

Giant components mixing fetch, validation, layout, and animation

Using any without explicit justification

Creating pages before the feature component system exists

END OF frontend.mdc
